<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<kml xmlns="http://www.opengis.net/kml/2.2">
  <Document>
    <name>🧊 LIVE ICE TYPE</name>
    <description><![CDATA[<h2>LIVE ICE TYPE</h2><p><b>Data time:</b> NIC analysis date 2026-05-16 (from NIC filename; retrieved 2026-09-23 11:21 AM EDT)<br/><b>Source updated:</b> unknown (NIC sends none)<br/><b>Source version:</b> nic-2026-05-16-5548ec16696d<br/><b>Processed:</b> 2026-09-23 11:21 AM EDT<br/><b>Status:</b> LATEST AVAILABLE (daily analysis, seasonal product)</p><p><b>Legend</b><br><img src="https://wyattleathorn7.github.io/great-lakes-live-environment/ice_type/legend.png?v=nic-2026-05-16-5548ec16696d" width="600" alt="legend"><br>Ice type = predominant WMO stage of development per analysis polygon:<br/><span style="background:#C9A227;">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;</span> 70 — Brash Ice (thickness not coded in SIGRID-3)<br/><span style="background:#176B5B;">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;</span> 80 — No Stage of Development (unstaged)<br/><span style="background:#DFF6FF;">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;</span> 81 — New Ice (<10 cm)<br/><span style="background:#BDE0FE;">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;</span> 82 — Nilas, Ice Rind (<10 cm)<br/><span style="background:#48CAE4;">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;</span> 83 — Young Ice (10-<30 cm)<br/><span style="background:#7D8597;">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;</span> 84 — Grey Ice (10-<15 cm)<br/><span style="background:#E0EAF5;">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;</span> 85 — Grey-White Ice (15-<30 cm)<br/><span style="background:#1D3557;">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;</span> 86 — First-Year Ice (>=30-200 cm)<br/><span style="background:#3A0CA3;">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;</span> 87 — Thin First-Year Ice (30-<70 cm)<br/><span style="background:#7209B7;">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;</span> 88 — Thin First-Year Ice Stage 1 (30-<50 cm)<br/><span style="background:#B5179E;">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;</span> 89 — Thin First-Year Ice Stage 2 (50-<70 cm)<br/><span style="background:#F72585;">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;</span> 91 — Medium First-Year Ice (70-<120 cm)<br/><span style="background:#D00000;">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;</span> 93 — Thick First-Year Ice (>=120 cm (open-ended; 150 cm assumed))<br/><span style="background:#370617;">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;</span> 95 — Old Ice (WMO gives no thickness; no estimate made)<br/><span style="background:#9D0208;">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;</span> 96 — Second-Year Ice (WMO gives no thickness; no estimate made)<br/><span style="background:#FFBA08;">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;</span> 97 — Multi-Year Ice (WMO gives no thickness; no estimate made)<br/><span style="background:#FFFFFF;">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;</span> 98 — Glacier Ice (not applicable (icebergs))<br/><span style="background:#2B2D42;">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;</span> unknown — Unknown / Undetermined (codes 99, -9)<br/>Open water is transparent; fainter = partial concentration.</p><p><b>Source:</b> U.S. National Ice Center (NAIS) daily Great Lakes ice analysis, SIGRID-3 shapefile<br/><a href="https://usicecenter.gov/File/DownloadCurrent?pId=35">https://usicecenter.gov/File/DownloadCurrent?pId=35</a></p><p>Transparent outside valid water data so existing project layers (shipwrecks, lighthouses, harbors, parks) stay visible. Turn on/off independently of the other ice, wave and temperature layers.</p><p><i>Not endorsed by NOAA.</i></p>]]></description>
